00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.400
So studying the interactions
of these two diseases

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.830
is really important for
improving the health outcomes

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:32.070
of these communities
where these diseases have

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:33.647
such a disproportionate effect.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:40.520
Okay, so now I'm gonna
go into some definitions.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:43.490
So I'll kind of define what diabetes is

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:46.770
and then talk about
what comorbidities are.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.520
And then lastly, I'll go
into significance testing,

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.593
which is a technique that is
used all throughout the study.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:57.610
And here are just some
keywords to keep track of

00:08:57.610 --> 00:08:59.870
while I'm talking about some definitions.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.690
So, the first is glucose.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:03.480
Second is comorbidity.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.690
And then the third is a significance test

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:09.573
as well as P-value, which is
part of significance testing.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:15.520
Okay, so what is diabetes?

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:18.450
So a really general
definition of diabetes is that

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:22.280
it is a metabolic disorder
where the body's ability

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:25.030
to process glucose is disrupted.

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:27.700
And I've kind of highlighted
these two really key terms

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:30.600
here, metabolic and glucose.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:34.600
And what metabolic means
is it pretty much refers

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:38.910
to any process that is kind
of biochemical in the body

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:42.780
which serves to sustain life in the body.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:45.310
And then glucose is just
a small sugar molecule

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:49.120
which gives energy to your cells.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.740
So kind of using those two definitions.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:56.680
Diabetes is a disorder where
the life sustaining processes

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:00.010
that relates to glucose
processes in your body

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:01.263
are somehow disrupted.

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:07.110
Okay, so now to better
illustrate on diabetes,

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:11.350
I'm just gonna talk about what
metabolism kind of looks like

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:15.620
in someone that is a non-diabetic.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:17.650
So, here's me.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:22.650
So let's say that one day
I decide to eat a pizza.

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:26.100
So as I eat this pizza,
it'll travel down my body

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:28.860
into my digestive system,
and it'll be broken down

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:32.373
into several different components,

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:37.530
some of which are
carbohydrates, proteins, fats,

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:40.630
and several other nutrients

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:45.250
which I need to sustain my metabolism.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:50.290
And so the one of these that's
really the most relevant

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.920
to our discussion today are carbohydrates,

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.950
which you can really think of as molecules

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:59.510
which kind of give your body
sort of like instant energy

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:02.163
after you eat food.

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:08.660
So once this pizza is broken
down into these carbohydrates,

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:13.070
the way that your body
can utilize the energy

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.710
from these carbs is by
breaking them further down

00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:19.350
into the glucose molecules
that I talked about before.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:23.790
And again, glucose is just
a small sugar molecule

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:25.933
that your body can use for energy.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:29.870
Okay, so once this process happens,

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:31.980
the glucose kind of floats around

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:33.790
throughout your body in your blood,

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:36.040
and it's just kind of
waiting to be absorbed

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:38.593
by your cells so that they
can use them for energy.

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:42.810
But the problem is,
glucose actually can't be

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:44.950
directly absorbed by cells.

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:48.790
The glucose can't penetrate
the cell right away.

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:51.710
So what the body does is it utilizes

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:54.420
a specialized organ called the pancreas.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:57.640
And what the pancreas does
is it can basically sense

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.450
when there's a lot of glucose in the body

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:02.300
after you just ate a meal, for example.

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:06.730
And then it releases a
hormone called insulin.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:09.640
So once insulin is
released into your body,

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.260
it attaches to your cells,

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:15.240
and what happens is it acts kind of like

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:17.680
a key to like a locked door.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:20.560
And then once this
happens, the glucose can

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:24.913
then absorb into the
cell and give it energy.

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:32.730
So what happens in the case
of a person that has diabetes?

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:36.730
So one of two things, or
actually both, can happen

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:37.920
in the case of diabetes.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:40.680
The first is called insulin resistance.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.840
And essentially what this is,

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:46.950
is even when insulin is
released into your body

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:50.330
and attaches to your cells,
the glucose that's floating in

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:53.490
your blood, it still
can't enter into yourself.

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:56.290
And then what happens is, your cells

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:58.110
basically can't get energy from glucose

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:01.430
and then the glucose starts
to accumulate in your blood.

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:03.573
The second thing is insulin misproduction.

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:08.340
And this basically happens
as a result of some damage

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:10.300
that comes from somewhere to the pancreas

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:15.120
and then it's basically unable
to produce enough insulin

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:18.510
basically, to meet the
demands of glucose absorption

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:19.453
for your body.

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:24.030
Okay, so when a person has diabetes,

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:26.030
and they develop insulin resistance

00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:28.330
and insulin misproduction,

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:31.410
what can happen is, since your cells

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:34.070
basically aren't absorbing the glucose

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:36.103
that you get from eating in your body,

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:39.580
the glucose starts to
accumulate in your blood,

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:43.093
and this can really cause a
lot of damage to blood vessels.

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:46.810
Then, again, since the cells

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:49.320
aren't absorbing enough glucose,

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.230
they may not have enough energy

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:54.650
to kind of do the typical processes

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:57.120
that they would typically be doing.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:59.900
And over time, this can cause damage

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:01.730
to a lot of organs throughout your body

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:05.670
and eventually cause
disease to those organs

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:08.460
and the most common as a result of

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:12.060
your cells not absorbing enough glucose

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:14.593
are kidney, liver and heart disease.

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:18.450
And all of these issues
given enough time can lead

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.453
to nerve degradation, excuse me,

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:25.050
which it itself can cause

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:28.313
a plethora of issues given enough time.

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:34.200
Okay, so, the last thing I wanna mention

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:37.040
about diabetes is that
there are two main types.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:41.380
So the first type, usually
called type 1 diabetes,

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.790
is one that typically comes about

00:14:43.790 --> 00:14:45.973
as a result of genetics.

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:50.060
And it's typically a result of some issue

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:55.060
in the cells of the pancreas
which produce insulin.

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:58.420
And this type of diabetes
accounts for approximately 5-10%

00:14:58.420 --> 00:14:59.923
of all diabetes cases.

00:15:01.730 --> 00:15:04.550
Okay, the second type of diabetes

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:06.940
is usually called type 2 diabetes.

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:08.870
And this type of diabetes
can really result

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:10.913
from a plethora of causes.

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:13.910
Poor diet can be a factor,

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:17.130
damage the pancreas can be a cause,

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:18.710
as well as genetics as well.

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:22.360
And this type of diabetes

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:25.200
is commonly called adult onset diabetes,

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:28.920
because it usually manifests in adults.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:32.390
And this accounts for approximately 90-95%

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:36.470
of all diabetes cases, and
is the type of diabetes

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:38.943
that we'll be discussing
for the rest of this talk.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:45.160
Okay, so now I really wanna
define what a comorbidity is.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:47.930
And this is basically just any condition

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:51.890
whose symptoms can overlap
and potentially exasperate

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:53.590
the symptoms of another condition.

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:58.010
So, a pretty easy example, to think about

00:15:58.010 --> 00:15:59.440
when thinking about comorbidities

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:03.590
is maybe imagine someone
that's suffering from asthma.

00:16:03.590 --> 00:16:04.770
So they have symptoms,

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:07.950
which may cause them to
have trouble breathing.

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:11.780
And then let's say that they
contract respiratory illness

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:13.970
like the cold or flu.

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:17.520
So the symptoms of that
respiratory illness could

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:20.440
potentially exasperate
the pre-existing asthma

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:22.310
that this person has.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:25.330
And as you can imagine,
breathing can become

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:26.390
much more difficult for them.

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:30.450
So we would say that asthma
has been a comorbidity

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:34.023
for that respiratory illness.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:40.410
So here, I'm just kind of
illustrating potential symptoms

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:43.660
that could cause comorbidities
for type 2 diabetes,

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:45.630
as well as COVID-19.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:48.190
So in the middle of the Venn diagram,

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:51.890
between the two conditions,
I've listed immune disruption,

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:54.840
vascular damage, as well
as multiple organ injury.

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:58.350
And these are kind of the
most severe outcomes of both.

00:16:58.350 --> 00:17:01.650
But as you can imagine,
the most severe symptoms

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:06.060
of conditions could lead to
the most severe exasperations

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:07.893
in terms of comorbidities.

00:17:10.340 --> 00:17:13.060
Okay, so now I kind of wanna pivot a bit

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:15.510
and talk about statistical significance

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:16.670
and significance testing,

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:21.143
which are two techniques that
are used throughout the study.

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:26.550
So first, statistical
significance is basically

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.920
a mathematical measurement
of the regularity

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:31.080
of some observed effect.

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:33.670
And then significance
testing are basically

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:35.610
any formal methods that are used

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:37.510
to determine statistical significance.

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:41.730
So, just a quick example
to kind of illustrate

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:43.940
these two concepts.

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:46.490
Let's say that we're
developing some medicine

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:49.580
and we wanna test if this medicine has

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:51.830
the desired effect that we think it does.

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:55.010
So, what we do is we give this medicine

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:59.220
to one group, labelled group 1, here,

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:01.680
and then we get placebo, which we know

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:04.280
definitely does not have
an effect to another group,

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:06.320
which is group 2, here.

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:08.530
And what we wanna do is do an experiment

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:12.570
where we basically compare
the effects of the medicine

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:14.800
on group 1 to group 2,

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:17.973
which we know the placebo isn't
having any effect on them.

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:24.100
So we do this experiment,
and then we collect the data,

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:27.350
and then we put it through
a significance test.

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:29.560
And what a significance test really is,

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:32.470
is just a mathematical
formulation, typically,

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:34.630
where the input is the data that you get

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:35.580
from an experiment.

00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:40.430
Okay, and what the
significance test gives you

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:43.330
is what is called a P-value.

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:48.030
And what you can really think
of a P-value is basically

00:18:50.441 --> 00:18:54.940
it's a probability that
describes or it's the probability

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:59.640
that the outcome that you
got from your experiment came

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:01.983
about as just pure chance.

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:04.140
So in this case,

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:07.513
from the experiments,

00:19:08.786 --> 00:19:11.330
let's say that we got that
the medicine actually did have

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:14.740
like some sort of effect on
group 1 as compared to group 2.

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:19.173
So, this corresponding P-value
we got, which is 0.045,

00:19:20.171 --> 00:19:24.510
or 4.5% is basically
saying that the probability

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:26.680
of the outcome that we
got, which is the medicine

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:28.630
actually did have an effect on group 1,

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:33.360
just being coming from
pure chance is very low,

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:36.643
or approximately 0.045 or 4.5%.

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:40.990
And so, given this P-value, we can say

00:19:40.990 --> 00:19:44.220
that we're pretty sure that
the results that we got

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:47.060
from this experiment is significant
and then we can say that

00:19:47.060 --> 00:19:49.590
we probably think that the medicine

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:53.193
actually definitely does
have a significant effect.

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:55.123
Okay.

00:19:56.630 --> 00:19:58.810
So, just a quick poll about P-values

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:01.480
to kind of drive this concept home.

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:05.910
So, the poll states, given
that a P-value represents

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:08.880
the probability that a phenomenon
happens by pure chance,

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:10.570
what P-value would we expect to get

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:12.920
from a significance test
something that most probably

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:15.920
is not a random occurrence?

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:19.150
And the answer choices are, a, 0.9 or 90%,

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:23.450
b, 0.001 or 0.1%,

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:25.890
c, 1 or 100%,

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:29.933
and finally, d, 0.67% or 67%.

00:20:38.913 --> 00:20:42.996
 

00:21:27.158 --> 00:21:28.550
 

00:21:28.550 --> 00:21:29.383
Excuse me.

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:32.820
All right, so looks
like most people said B,

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:35.770
which is the correct answer.

00:21:35.770 --> 00:21:38.510
And remember this is correct, because

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:41.230
the P-value represents the probability

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:43.260
that a phenomena happens by pure chance.

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:46.610
So if we're testing something
that we know is definitely

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:48.840
not a random occurrence,

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:51.390
we'd want the P-value to be extremely low

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:53.270
because that would tell us that

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:55.070
our intuition about that is correct.

00:21:57.150 --> 00:21:57.983
Okay.

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:02.573
So, going back to the keywords.

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:06.500
So, yeah, just keep these keywords in mind

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:08.410
as we move along in our discussion.

00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:10.250
So number one is glucose, which remember

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:14.090
is the small sugar molecule
which gives your cells energy.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:16.490
And then comorbidity, is any condition

00:22:16.490 --> 00:22:19.170
whose symptoms can overlap with another's

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:20.323
to exasperate them.

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:24.360
And then lastly, significance test,

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:27.860
which is basically any
formal test of significance,

00:22:27.860 --> 00:22:31.236
as well as the P-value,
which is the probability

00:22:31.236 --> 00:22:36.103
that the outcome that you
got came from pure chance.

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:39.490
Okay.

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:43.320
So now I'm going to go
into the methodology

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:45.693
that the authors used in this paper.

00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:47.423
Excuse me.

00:22:48.350 --> 00:22:50.450
So how do the authors approach this issue?

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:54.500
So before we get into the
the exact methodology,

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:56.700
I just wanna mention that
this is what is called

00:22:56.700 --> 00:23:01.530
a retrospective, longitudinal
and multicentered study.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:04.090
So what do these words mean?

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:06.790
So, a retrospective study is any one

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:08.220
that compares two groups of people

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:11.210
where the groups differ by one
or more factors of interest,

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:14.563
and where the data is collected
prior to the analysis.

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:17.430
And then a longitudinal study

00:23:18.268 --> 00:23:22.360
it's one where the same
observation basically

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:25.253
is made repeatedly over
a set period of time.

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:30.090
And then a multicentered study is just one

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:33.420
that takes place in multiple settings.

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:35.720
So keep these words in mind

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:37.930
and then I will come back to them

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:40.180
as I talk about the
methodology of the paper.

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:46.230
Okay, so this figure here kind of presents

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:50.220
exactly how the researchers
started their project.

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:54.520
So let's start at the top
of this flow chart up here.

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:59.520
So it says that 9,663 patients
were diagnosed with COVID-19

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:02.170
and we're admitted to 19
hospitals in the Hubei Province

00:24:02.170 --> 00:24:05.783
in China, from December
30th to March 20th.

00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:11.250
So, this is the initial cohort or group

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:14.150
of people that the
researchers started with.

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:17.860
And what they want to do
with this number of people

00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:21.120
is they want to split this
group into two groups,

00:24:21.120 --> 00:24:23.730
one being one with patients

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:25.480
that had pre-existing type 2 diabetes,

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:27.180
and then another one that did not.

00:24:28.150 --> 00:24:30.520
So, in order to get to these two groups,

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:32.890
the researchers took two or three steps,

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:35.130
where they essentially eliminated patients

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:37.230
who were ineligible for the study.

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:40.770
So for example, they
eliminated 872 patients

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:42.930
that did not have
complete medical records,

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:45.760
and therefore, could not
participate in the study.

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:47.940
Another example, about 1,000 patients

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:51.860
who were under the age of 18
or above 75, were eliminated

00:24:51.860 --> 00:24:56.860
and then finally, after
this elimination process,

00:24:56.960 --> 00:24:59.530
the researchers got to
this final cohort number

00:24:59.530 --> 00:25:03.440
of 7,337 total participants.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:07.900
Then they split this large group
up into two smaller groups,

00:25:07.900 --> 00:25:10.370
one that had 952 participants,

00:25:10.370 --> 00:25:13.560
and all of these patients had
pre-existing type 2 diabetes,

00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:18.330
and then at the other, which
had 6,385 participants,

00:25:18.330 --> 00:25:20.810
and this was the non-type
2 diabetes group.

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:22.940
And by the way, type two diabetes

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:24.327
is abbreviated here as T2D.

00:25:27.927 --> 00:25:29.873
Okay, so once the group had

00:25:33.415 --> 00:25:36.480
these two cohorts, what they did was

00:25:37.750 --> 00:25:41.410
they collected several
variables for all the patients.

00:25:41.410 --> 00:25:46.410
So, first they collected each
participant's age and gender.

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:51.220
Second, they collected
each participants symptoms

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:53.950
upon admission, as well as over time.

00:25:53.950 --> 00:25:56.730
And then next, they
collected the comorbidities

00:25:56.730 --> 00:26:00.440
that were present at admission
other than type two diabetes.

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:04.360
And then they also
collected the blood profiles

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:07.283
of every patient upon
admission as well as over time.

00:26:08.350 --> 00:26:09.580
And blood profiles

00:26:09.580 --> 00:26:11.610
are basically kind of
like your blood work.

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:14.660
So they were just looking for
things in the patient's blood,

00:26:14.660 --> 00:26:16.470
which were relevant to the study.

00:26:16.470 --> 00:26:17.860
So for example, they were looking

00:26:17.860 --> 00:26:21.580
for glucose concentrations,
or insulin concentrations

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:22.580
and stuff like that.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:27.400
And lastly, they collected
the overall management

00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:29.150
of each patient while admitted.

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:31.660
And what management means is basically

00:26:31.660 --> 00:26:36.660
any medical intervention that
any patient had to undergo.

00:26:36.710 --> 00:26:39.480
So that could have been
things like administration

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:42.380
of certain medicines, onto the patients

00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:47.093
or the use of some medical
instrument like a ventilator.

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:49.963
And, by the way,

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:54.430
just to go back to the
previous three definitions,

00:26:54.430 --> 00:26:57.430
this study is first of
all a retrospective,

00:26:57.430 --> 00:27:00.410
because we're comparing
two groups here that differ

00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:03.530
by some variable that we're interested in.

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:06.530
In this case, it's type 2 diabetes.

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:09.790
And then it's longitudinal
because we're recording

00:27:09.790 --> 00:27:11.960
all of these variables
over and over again,

00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:14.340
basically over a certain period of time.

00:27:14.340 --> 00:27:17.360
And then it's multicentered
because it took place

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:20.260
in 19 different hospitals where
the patients were admitted.

00:27:22.410 --> 00:27:27.410
Okay, so going further, the
researchers kind of delineated

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:33.200
the 952 type 2 diabetes patients
further into two groups.

00:27:33.910 --> 00:27:36.970
So the first group was, it was labeled

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:38.900
the well-controlled BG Group,

00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:41.800
which BG stands for blood glucose.

00:27:41.800 --> 00:27:45.590
And in this group, were
patients that had glucose levels

00:27:45.590 --> 00:27:47.900
in their blood which were considered to be

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:49.640
in a healthy range.

00:27:49.640 --> 00:27:51.720
And here this is considered to be

00:27:51.720 --> 00:27:56.720
from 3.9 to 10 millimolar per
liter of glucose in the blood.

00:27:57.270 --> 00:27:58.903
And a millimolar per liter is kind of

00:27:58.903 --> 00:28:00.653
just a measure of concentration.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:03.690
And the other group was

00:28:03.690 --> 00:28:06.320
the poorly controlled blood glucose group.

00:28:06.320 --> 00:28:09.840
And this group had patients who

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:12.000
had what is considered

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:14.170
to be unhealthy levels of
glucose in their blood.

00:28:14.170 --> 00:28:15.490
And in this case, that's considered

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:17.840
to be above 10 millimolar per liter

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:19.823
of glucose in their blood.

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:23.070
And then once they had these two groups,

00:28:23.070 --> 00:28:25.540
they recorded all the same
variables that I had just talked

00:28:25.540 --> 00:28:27.913
about in the same slide for both groups.

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:35.080
Okay, so once the researchers
had all of these variables,

00:28:35.500 --> 00:28:38.140
what they did was they compared

00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:41.390
all the groups that they
had delineated to each other

00:28:41.390 --> 00:28:43.580
on the basis of each variable.

00:28:43.580 --> 00:28:46.500
So in this case, the
researchers are comparing

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:48.320
the type 2 diabetes cohort

00:28:48.320 --> 00:28:50.890
to the non-type 2 diabetes cohort,

00:28:50.890 --> 00:28:54.070
on the basis of medical
management, and in this case,

00:28:54.070 --> 00:28:56.683
it's the use of particular drugs.

00:28:57.520 --> 00:29:02.520
And in this case they recorded
the frequency with which

00:29:02.550 --> 00:29:06.570
each group had to be
administered these drugs.

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:09.760
So once they had all this
data, what they did was

00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:12.190
they applied a significance test to it

00:29:12.190 --> 00:29:15.300
and then they generated P-values

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:18.923
for each instance of variable.

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:23.770
And I'm just looking at
a more specific example,

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:27.620
we're gonna look at a drug
called systemic corticosteroids,

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:30.000
which is basically a drug that helps

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.890
with really severe immune responses.

00:29:32.890 --> 00:29:37.540
So, looking at the type 2
diabetes cohort, we can see that

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:40.090
these patients were prescribed this drug

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:44.740
at a rate of about 29.4%
compared to the patients

00:29:44.740 --> 00:29:47.170
of the non-type 2 diabetes cohort,

00:29:47.170 --> 00:29:52.130
which were prescribed this
drug at a rate of about 23%.

00:29:52.130 --> 00:29:54.820
Okay, so, we can clearly see that

00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:58.630
the type 2 diabetes patients
were prescribed this drug

00:29:58.630 --> 00:30:00.500
at a much higher rate

00:30:00.500 --> 00:30:05.500
than we're the non T2D patients.

00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:09.060
So now, we wanna establish
significance in this data

00:30:09.060 --> 00:30:10.553
and we wanna be able to say that

00:30:10.553 --> 00:30:13.160
this isn't happening just by pure chance.

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:16.170
So what the researchers
are doing is they're taking

00:30:16.170 --> 00:30:19.203
this data and they're putting
it into a significance test.

00:30:20.470 --> 00:30:23.110
And in this case, they
get that the P-value

00:30:23.110 --> 00:30:28.110
for this particular case
is actually below 0.001,

00:30:28.190 --> 00:30:30.250
or below 0.1%.

00:30:30.250 --> 00:30:32.750
And remember, a P-value is the probability

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:37.100
that your outcome came as a
result of just pure chance.

00:30:37.100 --> 00:30:40.180
So in this case, that
probability is extremely low,

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:45.180
which means that we can say
that in fact, this outcome

00:30:46.570 --> 00:30:47.990
is significant and didn't come about

00:30:47.990 --> 00:30:49.740
as a result of pure chance.

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:52.793
And therefore, we can
say that type 2 diabetes,

00:30:53.800 --> 00:30:57.040
having type 2 diabetes
and COVID-19 is correlated

00:30:57.040 --> 00:30:57.940
with a higher rate

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:01.960
of being prescribed
systemic corticosteroids

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:04.693
to deal with particular symptoms.

00:31:07.010 --> 00:31:11.030
Okay, so now we're gonna go
into kind of the results section

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:13.823
and what the authors add to this field.

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:20.520
Okay, so the first major
result of the study

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:22.970
is that type 2 diabetes is in fact

00:31:22.970 --> 00:31:26.730
a major comorbidity for COVID-19.

00:31:26.730 --> 00:31:27.563
And

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:31.520
here I presented a table where

00:31:33.310 --> 00:31:37.690
they present the outcomes,
or the most severe symptoms,

00:31:37.690 --> 00:31:40.240
that patients faced.

00:31:40.240 --> 00:31:42.340
So in this table, what they're
doing is they're comparing

00:31:42.340 --> 00:31:43.930
the type 2 diabetes cohort

00:31:43.930 --> 00:31:45.860
to the non-type two diabetes cohort,

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:47.923
on the basis of outcome.

00:31:49.180 --> 00:31:54.125
So, from this table, we can
see that for pretty much

00:31:54.125 --> 00:31:59.125
every severe kind of outcome,
the type 2 diabetes cohort

00:32:00.700 --> 00:32:04.040
are experiencing these
outcomes at a much higher rate.

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:07.580
So if we look at one specific
example, which is ARDS,

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:10.490
which stands for acute
respiratory distress syndrome,

00:32:10.490 --> 00:32:15.100
which is basically a really
severe respiratory outcome,

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:18.890
we can see that the type
2 diabetes patients suffer

00:32:18.890 --> 00:32:23.100
from this outcome at a
rate of almost 17% compared

00:32:24.730 --> 00:32:27.250
to the non type two diabetes patients,

00:32:27.250 --> 00:32:30.723
which suffered this outcome
at a rate of just 7%.

00:32:32.080 --> 00:32:36.410
And this result, when applied
to a significance test had

00:32:36.410 --> 00:32:39.853
a P-value of below 0.1% which again,

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:45.230
means that we can correlate
having type 2 diabetes

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:48.470
with a higher rates of ARDS.

00:32:48.470 --> 00:32:52.960
And this is true pretty much
for all of these outcomes,

00:32:52.960 --> 00:32:54.723
except this one,

00:32:56.340 --> 00:32:57.173
DIC, which stands

00:32:57.173 --> 00:32:59.830
for disseminated
intravascular coagulation.

00:32:59.830 --> 00:33:01.140
And this had a P-value

00:33:04.593 --> 00:33:05.760
of above 0.05.

00:33:07.077 --> 00:33:09.660
So we can't really say that this result

00:33:09.660 --> 00:33:12.720
is really significant,
but we can't really say

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:14.140
that it's not significant

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:16.800
from the significance test that was done.

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:18.950
And this could probably be due to

00:33:18.950 --> 00:33:21.550
just like the low number of people

00:33:21.550 --> 00:33:23.570
who actually suffer from this outcome.

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:24.913
But in general,

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:28.100
for the rest of the outcomes,

00:33:28.100 --> 00:33:31.810
the results were extremely
significant below 0.1%.

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:38.820
Okay, so the second major
results from the study

00:33:39.790 --> 00:33:44.210
was that control of glucose
in the blood is essential

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:46.930
to good outcomes in COVID-19 patients

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:49.123
with pre-existing type two diabetes.

00:33:50.217 --> 00:33:53.639
And the first kind of chart
that I'm gonna present

00:33:53.639 --> 00:33:57.700
for this results, is a similar
one to the previous one.

00:33:57.700 --> 00:34:00.410
So in this case, they are comparing

00:34:00.410 --> 00:34:02.880
the well-controlled blood glucose cohort,

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:05.180
to the poorly controlled
blood glucose cohort,

00:34:06.020 --> 00:34:10.120
on the basis of medication prescription

00:34:10.120 --> 00:34:13.920
for particular outcomes that they had.

00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:18.920
So again, for pretty much every
single medical intervention

00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:22.970
that's shown in this
chart, we can see that

00:34:22.970 --> 00:34:25.780
the poorly controlled
blood glucose patients had

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:28.923
to be administered all
of these interventions

00:34:28.923 --> 00:34:30.583
at a much higher rate.

00:34:31.490 --> 00:34:34.850
So again, let's look at
systemic corticosteroids.

00:34:34.850 --> 00:34:38.270
So looking at the poorly
controlled blood glucose group,

00:34:38.270 --> 00:34:40.820
we can see that they are
prescribed this at a rate

00:34:40.820 --> 00:34:44.800
of about 35% compared to 20% for

00:34:44.800 --> 00:34:48.813
the well-controlled patients.

00:34:49.990 --> 00:34:54.990
And this result had a
P-value of less than 0.1%,

00:34:55.060 --> 00:34:59.790
which means that this results
was extremely significant.

00:34:59.790 --> 00:35:04.540
And this is true pretty
much for all of these drugs.

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:07.860
Some of them don't have the
most significant P-value,

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:11.710
for example, this one
has a P-value of 0.5%,

00:35:11.710 --> 00:35:15.710
which means that the probability
of this outcome being

00:35:15.710 --> 00:35:19.440
from pure chance is 50%,
which is extremely high.

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:21.100
So we really can't say much about this.

00:35:21.100 --> 00:35:24.090
But again, there wasn't
really many prescriptions

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:25.290
of this particular drug,

00:35:26.630 --> 00:35:28.640
seven for the poorly controlled group

00:35:28.640 --> 00:35:30.150
and then two for the
well-controlled group.

00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:33.800
So that really high P-value
could just come down

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:36.530
to not enough people participating

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:37.930
in that particular variable.

00:35:40.160 --> 00:35:45.160
Okay, so the next thing
I'm gonna present is

00:35:45.200 --> 00:35:49.291
this graph, which I think
really kind of encapsulates

00:35:49.291 --> 00:35:52.300
the result that controlled glucose

00:35:52.300 --> 00:35:56.490
is really, really important
to good outcomes in COVID-19

00:35:56.490 --> 00:35:58.193
and type 2 diabetes patients.

00:35:59.380 --> 00:36:02.160
So what this graph

00:36:03.270 --> 00:36:06.810
is kind of showing is the
percent survival rates for

00:36:08.700 --> 00:36:11.180
the well-controlled cohort patients,

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:13.700
as well as the poorly
controlled cohort patients.

00:36:13.700 --> 00:36:16.860
So first to kind of explain the graph,

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:20.340
on the x-axis is given time in days.

00:36:20.340 --> 00:36:22.783
So from day zero to day 28.

00:36:24.080 --> 00:36:28.530
And then on the y-axis is
given the percent survival

00:36:28.530 --> 00:36:30.210
and these numbers are in percents.

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:34.700
So it starts from 100%,
and it goes to 80%.

00:36:34.700 --> 00:36:39.700
So, first looking at the
well-controlled cohort,

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:42.603
we can see that over time,

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:46.950
their survival rate kind
of is pretty steady,

00:36:46.950 --> 00:36:49.860
it kind of hovers around about 98%,

00:36:49.860 --> 00:36:52.910
which is pretty comparable to people

00:36:52.910 --> 00:36:54.880
who actually don't have type two diabetes

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:57.143
who also contracts COVID-19.

00:36:58.070 --> 00:37:00.470
But then, when we look at

00:37:00.470 --> 00:37:04.140
the outcomes for the
poorly controlled cohort,

00:37:04.140 --> 00:37:06.250
we can see that over time,

00:37:06.250 --> 00:37:08.840
their survival rate actually dips down

00:37:08.840 --> 00:37:12.450
really dramatically compared
to the well-controlled cohort.

00:37:12.450 --> 00:37:17.140
It dips down to below 95% to about 93%

00:37:17.140 --> 00:37:18.393
at day 28.

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:23.490
And this is, I think, the
most significant results

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:27.480
of the study because
it really demonstrates

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:31.760
that even though the poorly
controlled cohort patients

00:37:31.760 --> 00:37:33.920
from the last slide, we know that they had

00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:35.570
more medical interventions,

00:37:35.570 --> 00:37:37.400
than did the well-controlled group.

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:39.160
The well-controlled
group is still surviving

00:37:39.160 --> 00:37:40.420
at a much, much higher rate

00:37:40.420 --> 00:37:42.390
than the poorly controlled group.

00:37:42.390 --> 00:37:44.330
So this kind of implies that

00:37:44.330 --> 00:37:45.870
one of the most important things

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:49.590
in good outcomes for COVID patients

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:51.680
with a pre-existing type 2 diabetes

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:54.220
really is just

00:37:55.660 --> 00:37:58.350
good control of blood glucose,

00:37:58.350 --> 00:38:01.830
and remember, healthy blood
glucose in the study is defined

00:38:01.830 --> 00:38:05.543
between three and ten millimolar per liter.

00:38:07.221 --> 00:38:08.054
Okay.

00:38:10.120 --> 00:38:13.420
So finally, I'm gonna talk a bit about

00:38:13.420 --> 00:38:15.150
the conclusions of the study

00:38:15.150 --> 00:38:20.150
and the specific limitations,
as well as future studies

00:38:20.180 --> 00:38:21.480
for this kind of research.

00:38:23.710 --> 00:38:25.730
So first, I'm gonna talk a bit about

00:38:25.730 --> 00:38:28.760
the clinical significance of the results.

00:38:28.760 --> 00:38:31.210
So this is kind of a cartoon,

00:38:31.210 --> 00:38:33.120
actually from the paper itself,

00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:35.330
and I think it really presents the

00:38:35.330 --> 00:38:39.780
most important clinical outcome
from the study really well.

00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:43.300
So it kind of depicts the previous graph

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:44.490
in a kind of cartoonish way.

00:38:44.490 --> 00:38:48.750
So the blue line is the survival rates

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:51.653
for the well-controlled
blood glucose cohort group.

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:57.010
And then the red line
represents the survival outcomes

00:38:57.010 --> 00:39:00.333
for the poorly controlled
blood glucose patients.

00:39:01.660 --> 00:39:06.220
So what this is kind of depicting is

00:39:06.220 --> 00:39:09.760
on the blue line, is
kind of a person who is

00:39:10.680 --> 00:39:13.570
like battling COVID kind of really well.

00:39:13.570 --> 00:39:17.020
And they have a survival rate of 98.9%,

00:39:17.020 --> 00:39:21.230
which again, is really
comparable to COVID patients

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:23.460
who don't have type 2 diabetes.

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:24.293
So

00:39:25.423 --> 00:39:27.710
and this I think, really demonstrates

00:39:27.710 --> 00:39:31.800
the importance of maintaining
good blood glucose

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:33.900
when diagnosed with COVID-19.

00:39:33.900 --> 00:39:38.900
And then, in comparison, on
the red poor outcome line,

00:39:39.890 --> 00:39:41.960
is a person is depicted
as kind of like falling

00:39:41.960 --> 00:39:44.870
as result of COVID-19.

00:39:44.870 --> 00:39:49.563
And these individuals have
a death rate of about 11%,

00:39:49.563 --> 00:39:52.550
which is really, really
a dramatic decrease

00:39:52.550 --> 00:39:55.860
from the 98.9 survival rate of those

00:39:55.860 --> 00:39:58.390
who have well-controlled blood glucose.

00:39:58.390 --> 00:39:59.363
So again,

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:03.210
the clinical significance here is that

00:40:04.350 --> 00:40:07.840
really one of the most important
things in these individuals

00:40:07.840 --> 00:40:11.500
who have COVID-19, as well as
pre-existing type 2 diabetes

00:40:11.500 --> 00:40:15.533
is really strict control
of their blood glucose.

00:40:18.130 --> 00:40:21.490
Okay, so now I'm gonna go
a bit into the limitations

00:40:21.490 --> 00:40:25.150
of the study, as well as
some future directions

00:40:25.150 --> 00:40:26.483
of this research.

00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:30.400
So first, a really good way

00:40:30.400 --> 00:40:32.530
that this research could be extended

00:40:32.530 --> 00:40:35.140
is through the performance of a similar

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:38.760
what's called a prospective
study, in order to supplement

00:40:38.760 --> 00:40:41.570
the current study, which is
again, a retrospective one.

00:40:41.570 --> 00:40:42.830
And what a prospective study is,

00:40:42.830 --> 00:40:45.610
it's really similar to
retrospective studies,

00:40:45.610 --> 00:40:49.170
except that when you're
comparing your two cohorts

00:40:49.170 --> 00:40:52.480
that have some variable
that you're interested in,

00:40:52.480 --> 00:40:55.810
what you're doing is you
are kind of tracking them

00:40:55.810 --> 00:40:58.900
or tracking outcomes, excuse me,

00:40:58.900 --> 00:41:03.900
in real time, rather
than working with data

00:41:03.900 --> 00:41:06.800
that was previously collected
as in a retrospective study.

00:41:08.840 --> 00:41:11.360
Okay, another thing that could be done

00:41:11.360 --> 00:41:14.633
is to increase the type
2 diabetes cohort size.

00:41:15.850 --> 00:41:18.270
And if you remember, the total cohort size

00:41:18.270 --> 00:41:20.850
was approximately 7000 patients,

00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:24.697
and then the type two diabetes
cohort size was about 900,

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:29.560
which is really, really
small, obviously, compared

00:41:29.560 --> 00:41:31.640
to the total cohort size.

00:41:31.640 --> 00:41:35.270
And size of the cohort really matters

00:41:35.270 --> 00:41:38.460
in these kinds of analyses,
because the statistics

00:41:38.460 --> 00:41:42.410
that the researchers take
advantage of in this case,

00:41:42.410 --> 00:41:45.010
really depend heavily on size.

00:41:45.010 --> 00:41:50.010
And when comparative size, as
in this case is really small,

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:53.970
we can get a lot of what's
called confounding factors

00:41:53.970 --> 00:41:56.650
in our analysis, which are

00:41:56.650 --> 00:41:58.050
basically just

00:41:59.760 --> 00:42:02.250
factors that we don't
necessarily care about

00:42:02.250 --> 00:42:05.253
but are affecting our results.

00:42:08.050 --> 00:42:11.300
Okay, so another thing that
we could do is to understand

00:42:11.300 --> 00:42:14.180
the comorbidity between COVID-19

00:42:14.180 --> 00:42:17.320
and type 2 diabetes at a
more fundamental level.

00:42:17.320 --> 00:42:21.050
And what I mean by fundamental is more of

00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:23.380
at a kind of basic science level.

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:26.440
So understanding the comorbidity
at like a biochemical level

00:42:26.440 --> 00:42:31.440
at a biophysical level, so that
we could potentially create

00:42:32.410 --> 00:42:36.770
a really specified specific treatments

00:42:36.770 --> 00:42:38.910
for individuals who suffer from

00:42:38.910 --> 00:42:41.553
both type 2 diabetes and COVID-19.

00:42:42.472 --> 00:42:44.460
And this could manifest

00:42:44.460 --> 00:42:45.920
in pharmaceutical treatment,

00:42:45.920 --> 00:42:49.373
but it could definitely be something else.

00:42:51.770 --> 00:42:55.580
Okay, and the last thing
is to make this study,

00:42:55.580 --> 00:42:57.640
to extend the results is to make the study

00:42:57.640 --> 00:43:00.910
more multicentered,
and that to perform it

00:43:00.910 --> 00:43:03.560
in more diverse communities.

00:43:03.560 --> 00:43:06.173
And as I had mentioned previously,

00:43:07.700 --> 00:43:09.190
both COVID-19,

00:43:09.190 --> 00:43:12.350
as well as type 2 diabetes
affects certain populations

00:43:12.350 --> 00:43:14.810
really at a disproportionate rate.

00:43:14.810 --> 00:43:18.740
And if you remember
this, the current study

00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:21.070
that we're talking about now was done only

00:43:21.070 --> 00:43:23.843
in Hubei Province in China.

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:27.990
So performing a really similar study

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:30.850
to this one would really help

00:43:30.850 --> 00:43:35.190
in kind of getting better health outcomes

00:43:35.190 --> 00:43:36.390
for particular communities

00:43:36.390 --> 00:43:38.800
that are affected
disproportionately by both diseases

00:43:38.800 --> 00:43:42.630
because these communities
could potentially have

00:43:42.630 --> 00:43:44.490
really unique factors that affect them

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:46.690
which don't affect other communities.

00:43:46.690 --> 00:43:50.300
So again, just making it
more multicentered, would

00:43:50.300 --> 00:43:55.160
really help to impact
the positive health outcomes

00:43:55.160 --> 00:43:57.743
that could possibly come
out of this kind of study.

00:43:59.630 --> 00:44:03.650
And kind of just to further
that point, I'm gonna present

00:44:03.650 --> 00:44:08.250
some data about diabetes
diagnoses in the United States

00:44:08.250 --> 00:44:11.190
as delineated by race and ethnicity.

00:44:11.190 --> 00:44:15.360
So, if you look on the last three bars

00:44:15.360 --> 00:44:19.360
on this bar graph here, you
can see that Hispanic people,

00:44:19.360 --> 00:44:22.170
black people, as well as
Native American people

00:44:22.170 --> 00:44:26.870
are diagnosed with diabetes,
the most frequently out

00:44:26.870 --> 00:44:31.400
of any other race or ethnicity
in the United States.

00:44:31.400 --> 00:44:35.160
And then this data kind of
presents something similar

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:38.260
but with COVID-19, so it presents

00:44:38.260 --> 00:44:40.540
the percentage of deaths due to COVID-19

00:44:40.540 --> 00:44:44.060
as delineated again,
by race and ethnicity.

00:44:44.060 --> 00:44:46.950
So from this data, we can see that Black

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:48.090
and African American people,

00:44:48.090 --> 00:44:51.710
as well as Spanish and
Latino people make up a 14%,

00:44:51.710 --> 00:44:55.140
as well as a 19% respectively.

00:44:55.140 --> 00:44:57.140
And this is data from New York State.

00:44:57.140 --> 00:45:00.563
So this is their populations
in New York State.

00:45:01.580 --> 00:45:05.150
But they actually make up

00:45:05.150 --> 00:45:08.480
more than 50% of all total COVID deaths,

00:45:08.480 --> 00:45:13.480
despite making a much less than
50% of the total population.

00:45:13.980 --> 00:45:17.730
So just taking this
data as well as the data

00:45:17.730 --> 00:45:20.510
that I presented in the last
slide, and kind of going back

00:45:20.510 --> 00:45:23.023
to why this study is really important,

00:45:24.890 --> 00:45:29.890
because obviously, this is
extremely disproportionate.

00:45:30.470 --> 00:45:35.350
And, again, both COVID-19
and type 2 diabetes affect

00:45:36.350 --> 00:45:38.350
these communities of color
really, disproportionately.

00:45:38.350 --> 00:45:43.110
So this kind of study that kind of looks

00:45:43.110 --> 00:45:45.760
into the interactions and intersections of

00:45:46.690 --> 00:45:47.770
both of these diseases,

00:45:47.770 --> 00:45:51.053
which again, affect these communities,

00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:54.240
at disproportionate rates
is extremely important

00:45:54.240 --> 00:45:57.740
to developing really
positive health outcomes,

00:45:57.740 --> 00:45:59.590
as well as just providing better medicine

00:45:59.590 --> 00:46:01.630
to these communities.

00:46:01.630 --> 00:46:04.950
And again, going back to my

00:46:06.690 --> 00:46:10.470
thing about making this
study more multicenter again,

00:46:10.470 --> 00:46:13.720
these communities can
really suffer from factors

00:46:13.720 --> 00:46:15.380
that are really unique to them,

00:46:15.380 --> 00:46:17.520
which create these disproportionalities.

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:20.940
So making this kind of
study more multicentered

00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:21.830
and more focused

00:46:21.830 --> 00:46:25.180
on these communities
specifically, can really

00:46:27.700 --> 00:46:29.720
impact these communities in a positive way

00:46:29.720 --> 00:46:34.490
and again, create really,
really amazing health outcomes,

00:46:34.490 --> 00:46:38.110
as well as just improve medical treatment

00:46:38.110 --> 00:46:39.710
of these communities in general.

